<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Neo-Inca State</h1><p class="st-meta">1537 CE – 1572 CE · capital Vilcabamba</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last independent Inca state, which held out in the forested mountains of Vilcabamba for 35 years after the Spanish conquest of Cusco.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1536 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a>, installed by the Spanish as a puppet, rebelled and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> for months. After failing to retake the city, he withdrew in 1537 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitcos">Vitcos</a> and then Vilcabamba, a remote region in the montane forest northwest of Cusco, where he founded a new Inca state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From Vilcabamba, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a> waged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war against the Spanish and kept up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> rituals and court life. After his murder in 1544 by Spanish refugees, his sons ruled in turn. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayri-tupac">Sayri Túpac</a> accepted Spanish terms and left in 1558. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titu-cusi-yupanqui">Titu Cusi Yupanqui</a> negotiated with the Spanish, allowed missionaries and dictated an account of the conquest, the Instrucción, in 1570.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-alvarez-de-toledo">Francisco de Toledo</a> sent an expedition in 1572 that captured the last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru">Túpac Amaru</a>, who was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>. Vilcabamba was abandoned and forgotten until explorers, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiram-bingham-iii">Hiram Bingham</a> in 1911, rediscovered its ruins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/espiritu-pampa">Espíritu Pampa</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/neo-inca-state.jpg" alt="Neo-Inca State" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Made and uploaded by Huhsunqu. · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_the_Inca_Empire.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1537 CE – 1572 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vilcabamba</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manco Inca, Titu Cusi Yupanqui, Túpac Amaru</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Inca religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Quechua</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Peru</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vilcabamba, Neo-Inca State of Vilcabamba</dd></dl></aside></div>
